// The velocity a geometry pass writes (TAA1 step 4). The world and previous world of the draw come from its
// GpuScene primitive row (Common/GpuScene.glslh, GpuScenePrimitive::LocalToWorld / PrevLocalToWorld):
// PrevLocalToWorld == LocalToWorld for a primitive that did not move since the scene's previous frame or was not
// drawn then, so it carries camera motion only.
//
// DesertVelocity is the GLSL twin of Graphic::VelocityNdc (Engine/Graphic/View/Velocity.hpp). Both clip
// positions are UNJITTERED — the vertex stage computes them with ViewProjection / PrevViewProjection, never with
// the jittered matrix gl_Position uses (ViewFrame rule: velocity never carries the jitter). They travel to the
// fragment stage as clip positions, not as NDC: the w-divide must happen per pixel, after interpolation.

#ifndef DESERT_VELOCITY_GLSLH
#define DESERT_VELOCITY_GLSLH

// xyz = current minus previous NDC (z: the device depth, as UE's velocity carries it with VELOCITY_ENCODE_DEPTH, so
// a reprojection knows where the surface sat in depth last frame: the screen probe gather's history test,
// Common/ScreenProbeTemporal.glslh); w = THE OBJECT-MOTION FLAG, the draw's v_Surface.ObjectMoved: 1 when the object
// itself moves (its transform or its skeleton changed since the previous frame, a moving instance producer), 0 for a
// still object, whatever its vertices do (the foliage wind, a pixel depth offset) and for the sky the clear leaves.
// UE4 kept the same distinction by WHO wrote velocity: a moved primitive did, a WPO-only one did not, and TemporalAA's
// AA_DYNAMIC_ANTIGHOST reads only that ("dynamic"); here every surface writes its xy (motion blur and TSR want the
// wind), so the flag travels beside it. The one reader is TemporalAA.shader's IsDynamicPixel.
vec4 DesertVelocity( vec4 clip, vec4 prevClip, float objectMoved )
{
    return vec4( clip.xyz / clip.w - prevClip.xyz / prevClip.w, objectMoved );
}

// THE CAMERA'S MOTION OF A PIXEL NOTHING WROTE (the far plane where no sky draws - the sky draw writes its own,
// DesertSkyVelocity below: velocity 0 at reversed-Z depth 0). Such a pixel keeps the clear value (Graphic::kVelocityFormat, View/SceneViewState.hpp), so every
// reader that reprojects it derives the motion here, from the view direction alone: a point at infinity, kept
// homogeneous (w = 0 after the inverse), so no finite far distance is needed. clipToPrevClip is
// PreviousViewProjection * InverseViewProjection, both unjittered (UE View.ClipToPrevClip, Graphic::ViewFrame::
// ClipToPrevClip). The readers: TemporalAA.shader's sky branch, TSRDilateVelocity.shader and
// MotionBlurFlatten.shader (UE VelocityFlatten: the sky blurs with the world under a turning camera). Returns
// current minus previous NDC.xy, as DesertVelocity does.
vec2 DesertCameraVelocityAtInfinity( vec2 ndc, mat4 clipToPrevClip )
{
    const vec4 prevClip = clipToPrevClip * vec4( ndc, 0.0, 1.0 );
    if ( abs( prevClip.w ) <= 1e-6 )
        return vec2( 0.0 );
    return ndc - prevClip.xy / prevClip.w;
}

// THE VELOCITY THE SKY DRAW WRITES (UE: the sky pass outputs its camera motion into the velocity target, so no
// reader and no vendor upscaler needs a separate fix-up pass). @p skyClip / @p skyPrevClip are the UNJITTERED
// ViewProjection / PrevViewProjection applied to the pixel's world view ray as a point at infinity (w = 0 before
// the multiply: the camera translation drops out), interpolated from the vertex stage and divided here per pixel,
// as DesertVelocity's clip pair is. The same motion DesertCameraVelocityAtInfinity derives from depth and
// ClipToPrevClip; z = 0 (both ends lie on the far plane, reversed-Z depth 0) and w = 0 (the sky is not an object
// that moved, the object-motion flag stays clear).
vec4 DesertSkyVelocity( vec4 skyClip, vec4 skyPrevClip )
{
    if ( abs( skyClip.w ) <= 1e-6 || abs( skyPrevClip.w ) <= 1e-6 )
        return vec4( 0.0 );
    return vec4( skyClip.xy / skyClip.w - skyPrevClip.xy / skyPrevClip.w, 0.0, 0.0 );
}

#endif // DESERT_VELOCITY_GLSLH
